AB  - This study investigates the potential adoption (feasibility) of innovative finance tools such as blended finance, crowdfunding, venture capital, equity financing, and angel investment for small and medium enterprises (SMEs) in developing economies where critical gaps exist in alignment with SME growth-stage financing needs, adequacy of regulatory frameworks (governance), and adoption barriers. Using a systematic review of peer-reviewed articles and policy reports (2019–2024), we use innovation diffusion theory (IDT) and dynamic capabilities theory (DCT) to examine SME financing challenges. Key findings show that early-stage SMEs are most dependent on angel investors (42% of cases) and crowdfunding (38%), growth-stage SMEs rely on venture capital (55%) and equity financing (30%), while mature SMEs face illiquid capital markets; 72% of crowdfunding platforms operate in regulatory “gray zones”, and weak contract enforcement hinders 60% of venture capital deals; and 3) firm-level barriers include perceived complexity (80% of SMEs avoid venture capital), limited dynamic capabilities (only 35% track financing trends), and structural disparities (30% higher rejection rates for women-led SMEs). Based on this study, we call for stage-specific policies, regulatory sandboxes, financial literacy programs, and development finance institutions (DFIs) led blended finance to address the $5.2 trillion in unmet demand for SME financing.
